# UART-CIRC agent instructions

## Scope and design authority

These instructions apply to all work in this repository. Follow the design in
`UART-circular.md` and the verification requirements in
`UART-circular-test-plan.md`. Keep the RTL, tests, and documents consistent.

The target system clock is 75 MHz, the maximum intended UART rate is 4 Mbaud,
the frame format is 8N1, and the default circular FIFO depth is 512 bytes.
Use the documented full/empty, read-response, overflow, and reset contracts.

Target Altera MAX 10 10M16SAE144C8G, selected by the user. The user has authorized
Quartus compilation and arbitrary provisional I/O locations. Let Quartus assign
the pins automatically and clearly identify I/O constraints as provisional.
Verify MAX 10 device-support installation before running a target-specific build;
generic Quartus availability is not sufficient. Device-library preflight has
confirmed MAX 10 and the selected part in the installed Quartus database.

LogicLock is not required. The user explicitly permits ignoring license advisory
292013. Do not create LogicLock regions; disable use of its balancing constraints.
Keep that advisory visible in logs and distinguish it from unresolved warnings.
The user also permits deferring Quartus power warnings for now, including 16562
(single-supply power review) and 215044 (missing board thermal model). Keep their
logs and power estimates; they do not block this provisional implementation.
Do not treat those deferrals as completed board power validation.

Implement synthesizable RTL and self-checking testbenches in VHDL, using `.vhd`
source files. Use VHDL-2008 as the project language standard and compile with
ModelSim `vcom -2008`; configure Quartus for VHDL-2008 as well. Keep synthesizable
code within the subset supported by the installed Quartus version. Do not switch
the implementation to Verilog or SystemVerilog without user direction.

Optimize RTL for area and avoid wide combinational fan-in. Prefer explicitly
bounded counters, short constant comparisons, fixed shift wiring, shared
arithmetic, and FPGA carry chains. Reduce constant timing ratios at elaboration.
Preserve synchronous RAM inference without resetting the data array. Do not add
wide decoders, variable-index datapaths, or replicated arithmetic without a
documented need. Preserve the interface and exact sample timing when optimizing,
and rerun the simulation gate. Treat mapped area, physical fan-in, and timing
improvements as unmeasured until Quartus implementation reports are available.

Report LE usage and resource utilization from the first successful Quartus
synthesis/mapping pass before making further area optimizations. Preserve that
first passing resource snapshot with device, baud, settings, and source hashes.
Do not mix later optimization results into the first-pass report.

## Required tools and execution order

Use Intel Quartus for FPGA synthesis, mapping, fitting, and timing analysis.
Use ModelSim for RTL compilation, elaboration, and simulation. Do not substitute
another simulator or FPGA implementation tool without explicit user direction.

Follow this order:

1. Generate or modify RTL, testbenches, and reproducible tool scripts.
2. Compile all relevant RTL and testbenches in ModelSim, then elaborate every
   required test configuration. Resolve all errors and warnings.
3. Run the applicable self-checking simulations, assertions, regression cases,
   and coverage checks from the test plan. Resolve all failures and warnings.
4. Record evidence that the code and simulation gate below has passed.
5. Only then run Quartus Analysis & Synthesis, including FPGA mapping.
6. After clean synthesis, run the Quartus Fitter and TimeQuest timing analysis.
   Review resource use, inferred RAM, CDC constraints, and timing results.

Quartus project files, source lists, pin assignments, and timing constraints may
be prepared before the gate passes. Do not run synthesis, mapping, fitting, or
a Quartus full compilation before the gate passes. Do not use synthesis as the
first way to check generated RTL.

## Mandatory code and simulation gate

Generated or modified code must be complete, reviewable, and free of unresolved
placeholders in the paths being implemented. ModelSim compilation, elaboration,
and simulation must complete with zero errors, zero warnings, no failed
assertions, no scoreboard mismatches, and no test timeouts. Do not hide failures
or disable warnings or checks to make the gate appear clean.

Run the full applicable RTL regression before the first FPGA synthesis or mapping
run. Include the 75 MHz / 4 Mbaud configuration, documented baud mismatch and
phase sweeps, the 512-byte FIFO boundary cases, overflow, reset, and diagnostic
tests. Include the 115200-baud and small-FIFO configurations required by the test
plan. Any required untested case or unresolved coverage requirement leaves the
gate incomplete; do not silently waive it.

The passing evidence must apply to the exact source and parameter versions used
for implementation. Any later RTL, testbench, or functional parameter change
invalidates the gate: rerun affected checks and the applicable regression before
resuming synthesis or mapping. Investigate synthesis-driven RTL fixes through
ModelSim again before repeating implementation.

If a required tool, license, device library, or target setting is unavailable,
report the blocker and continue independent source or script preparation. Do not
skip the gate, claim unrun tests passed, or proceed to FPGA implementation.

## Reproducible scripts and evidence

Update `README.md` after every completed project stage, in the same work session
as the stage completion. Keep its current-status table, design targets, toolchain
details, and next step accurate. State what was completed, what checks actually
ran and their results, where evidence can be found, and what remains pending or
blocked. Update status after failed or blocked attempts as well so it does not
imply a stage passed. Record the status date when changing the status table.

Track documentation, tool discovery, RTL generation, ModelSim compilation and
elaboration, simulation regression and coverage, Quartus project preparation,
synthesis and mapping, fitting and timing analysis, and hardware validation as
distinct stages. Mark a stage complete only when its required checks pass.
Tool version checks do not count as passing simulation or synthesis. If changes
invalidate prior verification, mark the affected README stage as requiring a
rerun and reflect the gate's current status before proceeding.

Provide checked-in ModelSim batch scripts and Quartus project/build scripts when
implementing the design. Compile sources in explicit dependency order. Use the
ModelSim VHDL compiler (`vcom -2008`) and run simulations in batch
mode with `vsim -c` and `.do` scripts. Make compilation errors, assertion failures,
mismatches, and timeouts produce a nonzero process exit status. A zero simulator
exit status alone is not proof of success: require an explicit test pass result
and inspect warning/error summaries.

Keep generated work libraries and implementation outputs separate from source.
Record tool versions, source revision or source hashes (including uncommitted
changes), parameters, test names, seeds, results, and coverage status. Preserve
logs and reproduction commands for the latest verification set. Keep only the
latest complete verified simulation results: after a fresh passing gate, run
`sim/clean_results.ps1` to remove superseded runs. A verified set can span multiple
suite directories; preserve every directory referenced by the current gate.
Old baseline results and resolved failure artifacts need not be retained. Report
code/simulation gate status separately from synthesis, fitting, and timing status.

## Quartus implementation requirements

Use the selected device and automatically placed provisional I/O for the current
build. Record the chosen provisional I/O standard and delay assumptions. The
user's permission to use any I/O does not establish the eventual board pinout
or electrical timing requirements; obtain those details before board sign-off.

Constrain the clock to 75 MHz (approximately 13.333333 ns period). Apply and review
appropriate synchronizer attributes and asynchronous-input constraints without
masking synchronous internal timing paths. Inspect the 512-by-8 memory inference,
registered read latency, full/empty behavior, and any inferred latches. Resolve
implementation errors, warnings, and timing violations before reporting the FPGA
build as clean. Do not program hardware unless the user authorizes programming.
